Subject: different sites within multi-season occupancy model
Message-ID: <4uLDqP06__M.JRI1n3_tAwAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/4uLDqP06__M/m/JRI1n3_tAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

Hoping someone has a suggestion for this.  I have avian point count data from a 21 year period within the same forest stands.  But the first 10 years the points were placed in 4 locations, while in the following 11 years the points were placed in 3 locations, with no overlapping points.

Will I be able to fun multi-season occupancy models given the survey points changed across time, even though all data was collected  in the same forest stands?  If so, just fill in the missing point info with NAs?

Or, and I'm not sure if this would be accepted or would be biased and/or a misuse of these models, would it be considered acceptable to just structure the models with the sites being the stands instead of the points?  Certainly some birds would be more likely to be detected within the entirety of the stand versus individual points, but it would remove the site differences.

Thoughts?

Thanks,

Rebecca

From u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com  Mon Oct  3 08:13:57 2022
From: u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com (Marc Kery)
