Thanks, Marc!

To be clearer about the objectives of the analysis, we wanted to test the relative role of the niche selection (deterministic factors), stochastic (ecological drift), and dispersal limitation on variations in bat species composition. In my view, including elevation in N-mix models will inevitably cause the role of deterministic factors to be exaggerated by further analyses.

I did some quick simulations with single-species N-mix in unmarked and noticed a lower bias with negative binomial distribution. Do you think that assuming a negative binomial distribution for lambda would lessen the negative effects of not including elevation in the model?
Besides, I also noticed that in a paper (Journal of animal ecology, 2010), you and Royle adopted a normally distributed site random effect "to account for spatial variation in abundance that is not captured by covariates". What is your current opinion on this?

Carlos

